deep.reconstruction:
Folio 4v is not a mathematical working sheet. It is the outer address leaf of a letter sent to Gauss. The address reads 'Herrn Hofrath Gauss Wohlgeb. à Göttingen'. It carries a NORTHEIM postal stamp, a '26 MAY' stamp and an inverted '25 MAY' stamp. Northeim is the post station near Göttingen. Gauss later turned the sheet upside down and used the blank right-hand panel for scraps.

(a) Non-mathematical jottings:
- the place name 'Grodno';
- the years 1821, 1822, 1823, 1824 written in a column;
- the numbers '26 27 / 26';
- the names 'Daussy' and 'Dawes', one 'Dawes' struck out, with 'D 3.4.5' and 'De'. These are presumably the hydrographer-astronomer Pierre Daussy and the double-star observer W. R. Dawes, so the notes are astronomical or bibliographic memoranda;
- the word 'Grand' twice;
- 'Ungeduldiger(?)' and 'Dieu soit loué'.

(b) A small numerical scrap, read in the inverted orientation (my reading, partly uncertain):
- a row of quotients 2/1, (4·3)/(1·2), (6·5·4)/(1·2·3), (8·7·6·5)/(1·2·3·4). These equal 2, 6, 20, 70, the central binomial coefficients C(2n,n) for n = 1 to 4. The '9' read in '6 5 9' is most likely a 4 seen upside down or written cursively; 6·5·4/6 = 20 fits the pattern.
- a row beginning 1, 3/1, 10/3, … . This is plausibly 1, 3, 10, 35 = C(2n-1,n), or the successive ratios of such coefficients. The entries '65' and '7.8 / 4.4' are too uncertain to reconstruct.
- isolated numbers '5 9', '13', '3 10', '10 4/7'.

No derivation is present. Nothing visibly connects these figures to the lemma-type counting argument (multiples aA lying in quarter-intervals of 4n, a ≡ 1 mod 4) on the previous page. Nor is any connection visible to the frequency table on the next page; I make no such claim.
